from assessment_writer import write_review
def compact(sid,title,rows,appraisal):
    write_review(sid,title,[(a,b.split(' | '),c) for a,b,c in rows],appraisal)

compact('MF-STAMPEDE-2025','STAMPEDE: metabolic improvement without a demonstrated overall survival benefit',[
('Objectives; appendix SAP 4.0 §§5,6','Test adding metformin to standard care for metastatic hormone-sensitive prostate cancer. | Primary overall survival; metastatic and nonmetastatic cohorts analyzed separately under SAP. | Survival, progression and metabolic outcomes.','This report covers the metastatic cohort only.'),
('Methods; Figure 1; Table 1','Men with metastatic prostate cancer without diabetes. | 112 UK/Swiss hospitals; HbA1c below 6.5%, eGFR at least 45, performance status 0–2. | 1874 randomized: 936 metformin, 938 standard care; median age 69; 94% newly diagnosed.','Separate nonmetastatic participants are not added to this denominator.'),
('Procedures','Oral metformin. | 850 mg daily for four to six weeks then twice daily, continued if tolerated. | ADT in all; 82% received docetaxel and only 3% an androgen-receptor pathway inhibitor. | Median follow-up 60 months by case forms, 69 months including civil registration.','Current treatment combinations may differ.'),
('Randomization and masking','Standard care alone. | Central telephone allocation 1:1 with balancing factors. | Minimization with 20% random component. | Open-label. | Concurrent randomized enrollment.','Mortality is less subjective than symptom reporting, but treatment and adverse-event reporting were unmasked.'),
('Outcomes; appendix SAP','Primary overall survival; progression and metabolic endpoints secondary. | Time-to-event models; laboratory measures and weight. | Survival over follow-up; selected metabolic comparisons at 104 weeks. | Survival clinical; glucose, lipids and weight intermediate outcomes. | NR: No universal patient-important threshold for the metabolic changes.','Metabolic improvement does not establish prevention of cardiovascular events.'),
('Figures 2–3; Table 2','Deaths 453 metformin versus 473 standard care; median survival 67.4 versus 61.8 months. | Randomized denominators 936 and 938. | Overall survival HR 0.91, 95% CI 0.80–1.03, P=.15. | At 104 weeks weight rose 2.00 versus 4.40 kg; adjusted contrast −2.48 kg in 583 participants. | Weight contrast CI −3.55 to −1.41 kg; high-volume survival HR .79 but volume interaction P=.12.','The high-volume subgroup does not establish a survival benefit confined to that subgroup.'),
('Figure 1; treatment delivery; appendix','42 withdrew follow-up consent, 22 versus 20. | Metabolic analyses use available subsets; baseline plus 104-week weight in 583. | 897 confirmed metformin starters; 28 stopped within 30 days; median stopping time 39 months. | 15 explicitly never started; another 24 assumed started because date missing. | ITT efficacy; 921 metformin safety versus 938 control. | Individual participant.','26 control participants started metformin; no pill-diary adherence measure.'),
('Table 3; appendix S6–S9','Routine adverse-event and serious-event reporting; CTCAE grading. | 921 metformin versus 938 standard care. | Grade 3+ events 523/921 versus 487/938; any diarrhea 600 versus 350; grade 3 diarrhea 46 versus 29. | 134 of 510 who stopped metformin cited excessive toxicity. | Serious events collected to progression in control and until progression plus at least 30 days after stopping in metformin.','Different observation windows and prolonged exposure affect harm comparisons.'),
('Discussion; appendix SAP §§5.14,6','Open-label design, missing metabolic measurements and treatment crossover. | Randomization supports overall contrast; availability-selected metabolic subsets may differ. | SAP version 4.0 dated March 19, 2024 inspected for primary and subgroup rules. | Many secondary/subgroup comparisons; high-volume PFS interaction is exploratory. | Overall survival interval includes no effect and modest benefit. | Appendix contents call protocol v21 but embedded protocol is v23 dated October 5, 2021.','The appendix naming discrepancy is retained; it is not evidence of a different result.'),
('Eligibility; Discussion','Metastatic hormone-sensitive prostate cancer without diabetes. | Long-term oral metformin with predominantly ADT plus docetaxel. | No overall survival benefit demonstrated; less weight gain observed. | Very few received contemporary AR-pathway inhibitors; no direct generalization to every regimen.','Not evidence to replace established cancer treatment.'),
('NCT00268476; appendix','STAMPEDE metformin comparison, arm K. | Other platform arms and nonmetastatic cohort are separate comparisons. | Shared platform does not make all publications independent metformin trials. | Current guide inventory, not exhaustive prostate-cancer synthesis.','Count this randomized comparison once.'),
('Byline; declarations; funding','Silke Gillessen and STAMPEDE collaborators. | MRC Clinical Trials Unit and participating UK/Swiss centers; UCL sponsor. | Cancer Research UK, Prostate Cancer UK and UKRI/MRC; funder approved design/amendments, not analysis. | NR: Product supply not confirmed in this targeted assessment. | Numerous disclosed investigator industry relationships; no inference of invalidity from funding alone.','The primary estimate governs the guide conclusion.'),
('Full report and 339-page appendix','Lancet Oncology 2025, DOI 10.1016/S1470-2045(25)00231-1. | Full report and appendix acquired. | Protocol and SAP included; targeted primary, subgroup and safety sections inspected, not every platform appendix page. | NCT00268476 and ISRCTN78818544. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved records. | November 3–4, 2026.','Do not label the targeted audit a complete audit of all platform documents.'),
('G-METFORMIN prostate-cancer section','Survival versus metabolic outcomes. | Supports less weight gain; does not establish overall survival benefit. | Observed metabolic results and authors’ subgroup hypotheses are presented separately.','No favorable subgroup headline.'),
('Synthesis','Metformin improved selected metabolic measures but did not significantly improve overall survival. | Effects with current regimens and prospectively confirmed subgroups remain uncertain. | Direct trials with modern therapy and clinical outcomes could change the conclusion.','Treatment decisions require the cancer team.')
], 'Large randomized evidence does not demonstrate overall survival improvement in the metastatic cohort. Metabolic effects are real intermediate findings in a smaller available-case subset. The high-volume survival subgroup lacks a convincing interaction and cannot overturn the primary result.')

compact('COND-C17-MET-2015','Advanced pancreatic cancer: metformin did not improve survival',[
('Summary; Methods','Test adding metformin to gemcitabine and erlotinib. | Prespecified six-month overall survival. | Clinical survival benefit in advanced pancreatic cancer.','Mechanistic hypotheses are secondary.'),
('Patients; Table 1; Figure 1','Adults with advanced pancreatic adenocarcinoma. | Four Dutch centers; approximately 73% metastatic; diabetes allowed. | 121 randomized: 60 metformin and 61 placebo; diabetes in six and eight respectively.','Not a prevention study.'),
('Procedures','Oral metformin tablets. | 500 mg twice daily in week one, then 1000 mg twice daily until progression or intolerance. | Gemcitabine 1000 mg/m² on days 1, 8 and 15 every 28 days plus erlotinib 100 mg daily. | Median follow-up 28.1 months.','Not representative of every current pancreatic regimen.'),
('Randomization and masking','Matched placebo with identical cancer treatment. | Central TENALEA allocation, stratified by hospital, diabetes and stage. | Computer-generated blocks of six; block size concealed. | Patients, physicians and study personnel masked. | Concurrent randomized phase II comparison.','Good allocation safeguards support the null comparison.'),
('Outcomes; statistics','Primary six-month overall survival; PFS, response and metabolic analyses secondary. | Survival follow-up and RECIST response; CTCAE toxicity. | Primary six months. | Clinical endpoints; insulin and drug concentrations exploratory. | Design sought a very large six-month survival change from 50% to 75%.','Power does not establish a small-effect equivalence margin.'),
('Results; Figure 2','Six-month survival 56.7% metformin versus 63.9% placebo. | 60 versus 61 randomized; 52 versus 54 deaths over follow-up. | Median survival 6.8 versus 7.6 months; HR 1.056, 95% CI .72–1.55. | Six-month difference −7.2 percentage points, calculated from rounded estimates. | Six-month P=.41; overall HR P=.78; PFS HR 1.18, CI .77–1.82.','No demonstrated survival advantage.'),
('Figure 1; treatment exposure','Follow-up continued after drug discontinuation. | Serial plasma analysis available in selected participants, including 29 metformin recipients. | Full-dose escalation in 38/60 metformin versus 49/61 placebo; dose reductions 34 versus 17. | No reported postrandomization efficacy exclusion from ITT. | Randomized ITT efficacy; selected biomarker subsets. | Individual patient.','Drug-concentration subgroups are not randomized dose comparisons.'),
('Safety; Table 3','Graded treatment-emergent adverse events. | 60 metformin and 61 placebo assigned. | Grade 3/4 neutropenia 15 each; diarrhea six versus three; fatigue six versus two; no hypoglycemia reported. | All study drugs stopped for toxicity in 13 versus eight; one metformin-only GI withdrawal and four metformin/erlotinib GI withdrawals. | Median treatment 2.5 versus 4.6 months.','Shorter exposure in metformin complicates direct toxicity-frequency interpretation.'),
('Methods; Discussion','Small phase II trial with good masking; limited exposure and dose reductions. | More performance-status-2 participants and higher CA19-9 in metformin arm; adjusted survival remained null. | GAP: Separate protocol/SAP not acquired for historical endpoint comparison. | Biomarker and concentration analyses exploratory and multiple. | Wide HR interval permits moderate benefit or harm. | One Results sentence calls placebo n=60; randomization figure and total establish 61.','Preserve the discrepancy; use coherent randomized denominators.'),
('Discussion','Advanced pancreatic cancer, including a small diabetic subgroup. | This chemotherapy combination and conventional antidiabetic doses. | Does not support a survival benefit as an add-on. | Not all cancers, preventive settings or future combinations.','Post hoc high-concentration survival cannot justify dose escalation.'),
('NCT01210911','Kordes pancreatic phase II trial. | Biomarker analyses are within this trial. | No additional independent trial from serial drug measurements. | Current guide sources only.','One randomized family.'),
('Byline; declarations','Sil Kordes, Michael Pollak, Johanna Wilmink and colleagues. | Academic Medical Centre and other Dutch hospitals. | Academic Medical Centre and Terry Fox Foundation; stated no funder role. | NR: Commercial product supply details not confirmed in inspected text. | Authors declared no competing interests.','Disclosure does not substitute for design appraisal.'),
('Published full report','Lancet Oncology 2015, DOI 10.1016/S1470-2045(15)00027-3. | Full report acquired from author-associated institutional source. | GAP: Separate protocol and appendices not recovered. | NCT01210911 identified in report. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved records. | November 3–4, 2026.','Primary results directly inspected.'),
('G-METFORMIN pancreatic-cancer section','Metformin as cancer treatment add-on. | Contradicts expected major survival benefit. | Trial data are null; mitochondrial or insulin explanations remain hypotheses.','Do not imply a responsive high-dose subgroup was proven.'),
('Synthesis','This regimen did not improve survival. | Small effects, different regimens and biologically selected groups remain separate questions. | Adequately powered randomized clinical outcome trials would be needed.','Not a reason to change prescribed diabetes care without clinical review.')
], 'Blinded randomized phase II evidence shows no survival benefit and more dose modification with metformin. Exploratory exposure-response observations cannot establish that higher doses work.')

compact('MF-ADENOMA-2016','Post-polypectomy adenomas: a promising small trial, not proof of cancer prevention',[
('Objectives; Methods','Test low-dose metformin against recurrence of colorectal adenomas and polyps. | Coprimary number and prevalence at one year. | Post-polypectomy lesion recurrence.','Not colorectal cancer incidence or mortality.'),
('Patients; Figure 1','Nondiabetic adults aged 40–80 after polypectomy. | Five Japanese hospitals; excluded regular NSAIDs, hereditary syndromes and major organ disease. | 498 screened; 151 randomized, 79 metformin and 72 placebo; 133 follow-up colonoscopies, 71 and 62.','About 70% had multiple or advanced adenomas at baseline.'),
('Procedures','Metformin tablets versus lactose placebo. | Oral 250 mg once daily after breakfast for one year. | Prior polypectomy; surveillance colonoscopy. | One year.','This unusually low research dose is not a prescribing recommendation.'),
('Randomization and masking','Identical placebo. | Central stratification by hospital, age, sex and BMI. | Computer-generated random allocation. | Patients, clinicians, endoscopists and investigators masked. | Concurrent 2011–2014 recruitment.','Experienced endoscopists and masked video review strengthen outcome ascertainment.'),
('Outcomes; statistical analysis','Coprimary lesion count and prevalence; metabolic measures secondary. | Colonoscopy, three-expert video review and pathology. | One-year repeat examination. | Adenoma/polyp recurrence is an intermediate cancer-risk endpoint. | NR: No established patient-important threshold for one-year lesion reduction.','Adenoma reduction is not itself cancer prevention.'),
('Table 2; Results','Any polyp 27/71 metformin versus 35/62 placebo; adenoma 22/71 versus 32/62. | Observed colonoscopy denominators 71 and 62, not all 151 randomized. | Polyp RR .67, 95% CI .47–.97; adenoma RR .60, CI .39–.92. | Any-polyp prevalence 38.0% versus 56.5%; median polyp count zero versus one. | P=.034 for polyp prevalence, .016 for adenoma prevalence; count comparisons P=.041 and .037.','Adenoma percentage printed as 30.6% does not reproduce 22/71; retain counts and reported RR.'),
('Figure 1; compliance','Eight metformin and ten placebo lacked follow-up colonoscopy. | Complete-case outcome analysis despite ITT terminology. | Median compliance 91% versus 92%; nobody removed for under-80% adherence. | 347 excluded before randomization, including 183 with concern about incomplete baseline clearance. | 133 with follow-up colonoscopy. | Individual participant; multiple lesions are not extra patients.','Missing outcome bias remains possible.'),
('Table 3; Results','NCI-CTCAE version 4.0 grading during follow-up. | Safety table uses 71 and 62 completers. | Six metformin and nine placebo events listed, all grade 1; diarrhea one versus four; no serious events reported. | One placebo discontinuation for diarrhea. | One year.','Small size and completer safety denominator cannot establish absence of rare harm.'),
('Discussion; methods','Small regional trial, incomplete colonoscopy follow-up and possible missed baseline lesions. | Randomization limits confounding; post hoc insulin-resistance groups are not randomized. | GAP: Separately published 2012 protocol not acquired in this packet. | Multiple coprimary/count/metabolic tests without a reported multiplicity adjustment. | Modest event counts, confidence intervals near no effect. | Reported adenoma 30.6% differs from 22/71; claimed ITT actually depends on observed follow-up.','HOMA-IR subgroup interaction P=.141 does not prove a predictive responder marker.'),
('Discussion','Selected Japanese nondiabetic post-polypectomy adults. | 250 mg/day, one year. | Supports possible reduction in recurrent lesions. | Not average-risk adults, longer-term cancer prevention, cancer treatment or all doses.','Surveillance remains a separate established care question.'),
('UMIN000006254; references','Higurashi 2016 randomized trial. | Hosono 2010 crypt-foci pilot is a separate earlier study. | Distinct participants; same research lineage does not mean independent replication of cancer outcomes. | Current guide inventory only.','Do not pool crypt foci, adenomas and cancer as one outcome.'),
('Byline; funding; declarations','Takuma Higurashi, Atsushi Nakajima and colleagues. | Five Japanese hospitals. | Japanese Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare cancer-research grant; stated no funder role. | Drugs purchased from Dainippon Sumitomo and Kokando. | Authors declared no competing interests.','Purchased products are not described as donated.'),
('Full published report','Lancet Oncology 2016, DOI 10.1016/S1470-2045(15)00565-3. | Full nine-page article acquired and read. | GAP: Separate protocol and any additional appendix not recovered. | UMIN000006254 identified. | No correction/retraction identified in retrieved records. | November 3–4, 2026.','The numerical discrepancy remains visible in the assessment.'),
('G-METFORMIN adenoma section','Adenoma recurrence versus colorectal cancer prevention. | Supports a preliminary lesion-reduction signal; cancer prevention remains unproven. | Authors’ broader chemoprevention interpretation exceeds the measured one-year cancer outcomes.','No colorectal cancers detected at follow-up does not prove prevention.'),
('Synthesis','A small blinded trial is encouraging for recurrent lesions. | Durability, generalizability, rare harms and cancer outcomes remain uncertain. | Larger independent long-term randomized trials with cancer outcomes could strengthen the case.','Do not recommend self-treatment or reduced surveillance.')
], 'Promising randomized evidence for one-year lesion recurrence in a selected population, limited by sample size, missing colonoscopies, multiplicity and surrogate outcomes. It does not establish reduced colorectal cancer incidence or mortality.')
